      'Because the ear can handle an enormous range of loudness, intensity is measured on a logarithmic scale, in decibels. Each rise of 10 dB means ten times more sound energy: a whisper is about 15 dB, conversation about 60 dB and a jet engine about 120 dB. Prolonged exposure to sounds above about 85 dB can damage hearing, and a very loud sound such as a gunshot, around 140 dB, damages the ear immediately and is painful. The ear is most sensitive between about 500 and 4000 Hz, which is the range of human speech.',
