      'The central problem of hearing is that sound arrives as vibrations in air but must be detected in the fluid of the inner ear. Fluid is much harder to move than air, so if sound in air simply hit the fluid, almost all of it would be reflected away, as most sound bounces off the surface of a swimming pool. The middle ear exists to solve this mismatch.',
